<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :v3_enhancement_guide %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:v3_enhancement_guide) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, '1. Make Your Plugin Theme-Ready') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">A theme recolors the whole screen. Your plugin does not load themes itself; it renders inside a screen that may carry one. A plugin is theme-ready when everything it draws uses framework classes and colors.</p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li>Style with framework utilities (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text--</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border--</code>) and elements (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">value</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title</code>). Themes recolor all of them.</li>
          <li>Avoid hardcoded hex colors and inline styles; a theme cannot recolor them.</li>
          <li>Test with the Style selector in the docs screen picker. It applies a theme to every example on the page.</li>
        </ul>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          To theme a screen you control, include the theme stylesheet and add the theme class. Note that <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen--dark-mode</code> has no effect on a themed screen; a theme already decides every color. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :themes) %>.
        </p>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '<link rel="stylesheet" href="plugins.css">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="themes/black-and-yellow-theme.css">

<div class="screen screen--theme-black-and-yellow">...</div>'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, '2. Migrate Charts to TRMNLCharts') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">Charts with hardcoded colors stay frozen while the rest of the screen adapts. The runtime bundles <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts</code>, a Highcharts adapter that takes its series colors, grid lines, and text styles from the screen.</p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li>Build your chart inside <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts.watch()</code> so it rebuilds when the device, scale, mode, dark mode, or theme changes.</li>
          <li>Start from <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts.options()</code> merged under your own settings.</li>
          <li>Color each series with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLCharts.series(i, n)</code> instead of a literal color.</li>
          <li>Convert numeric chart dimensions with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.px()</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: 'var el = document.getElementById("my-chart");

TRMNLCharts.watch(el, function () {
  var px = function (value) { return TRMNLPaint.px(value, { el: el }); };
  Highcharts.chart(el, TRMNLCharts.merge(TRMNLCharts.options({ el: el }), {
    chart: { height: px(260) },
    plotOptions: { series: { lineWidth: px(4) } },
    series: [
      { data: incoming, color: TRMNLCharts.series(0, 2, { el: el }) },
      { data: outgoing, color: TRMNLCharts.series(1, 2, { el: el }) }
    ]
  }));

  // Paint legend markers tagged data-chart-series="i" with matching series colors.
  TRMNLCharts.applySwatches({ el: el });
});', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          Full examples for line, multi-series, and bar charts are on the <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %> page.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, '3. Mark Monochrome Icons Adaptive') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">Add <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image--adaptive</code> to monochrome silhouette icons. The framework takes the icon's shape and repaints it in the screen's icon color, following bit depth, dark mode, Raw/Preview, and the active theme.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '<!-- Monochrome silhouette icons (shape on a transparent background) -->
<img class="image--adaptive" src="path to icon">'
          ) %>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li>Silhouettes only: never use it on photos or multi-color logos. Use <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image_stroke) %> to keep those legible instead.</li>
          <li>Icons must be same-origin or served from a CORS-enabled host, or the framework leaves them unpainted. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image) %>.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, '4. Resolve Paint from JavaScript') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">Drawing something yourself, on canvas, in SVG, or with another library? Ask <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint</code> for the colors. It returns what CSS would paint right now, with bit depth, dark mode, and the active theme already applied.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(demo_code: '// A background token: solid color on 4-bit+, dither tile on 1-bit.
var fill = TRMNLPaint.bg("gray-40", { el: "my-visual" });

// The effective one-color value of a text utility for SVG or canvas text.
var ink = TRMNLPaint.textColor("default", { el: "my-visual" });

// Rebuild whenever the screen device, scale, mode, dark mode, or theme changes.
TRMNLPaint.watch("my-visual", function () { draw(); });', language: 'javascript') %>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          The full resolver and painter surface is documented on <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_api) %>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, '5. Move Borders to the Step Rail') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">Replace the numbered border levels with shade steps; the numbered classes still render, but they are deprecated. Steps use the same 10 to 75 scale as backgrounds, and themes recolor them. Borders now render as generated gradients instead of PNG tiles, so a bordered screen fetches no images.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '<!-- Before: numbered levels (deprecated) -->
<div class="item border--h-5">...</div>

<!-- After: shade steps, plus semantic black/white rails -->
<div class="item border--h-45">...</div>
<div class="item border--h-black">...</div>'
          ) %>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :border) %> for the full step scale and how themes recolor it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-6">
        <div class="space-y-1">
          <%= framework_heading(3, '6. Keep Overlaid Text and Images Legible') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">Text and images placed over a shaded or patterned surface can lose contrast. The Text Stroke and Image Stroke utilities outline them, and both were rebuilt to follow themes, dark mode, and bit depth like the rest of the screen.</p>
        </div>

        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 <%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          <li>Add <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke</code> to framework text over a busy background, and size it with <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--small</code> through <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--xlarge</code>. The stroke renders as stacked drop shadows, so it stays behind the letters in every browser, even when the text is filled with a pattern.</li>
          <li>Add <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image-stroke</code> to a transparent or vector image for the same effect, with the matching <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--small</code> through <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">--xlarge</code> sizes.</li>
          <li>Leave the color off to stroke with the default contrast ink, or set one with a color variant (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--black</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image-stroke--white</code>, or any palette token). Color variants follow the theme, so themed and dark-mode screens recolor the outline to match.</li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '<!-- Framework text over a shaded background -->
<span class="value text-stroke text-stroke--medium">64%</span>

<!-- Transparent or vector image over a pattern -->
<img class="image-stroke image-stroke--large" src="path to icon">'
          ) %>
        </div>

        <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
          Full size and color scales are on the <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :text_stroke) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image_stroke) %> pages.
        </p>
      </section>

      <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
